<% content_for :title, "About · Provenance Pipeline" %>
<%# What the app is and how its parts work (4e): an opening, four steps, then (step 13) a tab for each part, with a
    sentence, a few points and its pages, at a readable line length. A step's link opens its tab. The pages' "How this
    works" folds say the same, where it applies. %>
<div class="about">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    An agent-governed CRM for a job search. AI agents do the legwork under scoped permissions, your AI assistant works
    beside them through MCP, and every change on record says who made it, under which permission, and why. The
    decisions that matter stay yours.
  </p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li><a href="#watch"><strong>Watch</strong><span>Roles checked at each employer's own careers page.</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#find"><strong>Find</strong><span>Roles and companies that fit, proposed for you to decide.</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#apply"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Your pipeline, the writing for it, and the people in it.</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#govern"><strong>Govern</strong><span>Credentials, the review queue, the audit log.</span></a></li>
  </ol>

  <div data-controller="tabs">
  <%= page_tabs([ [ "watch", "Watching" ], [ "find", "Finding" ], [ "apply", "Applying" ], [ "govern", "Governance" ],
                  [ "assistant", "AI assistant (MCP)" ] ], label: "How it works") %>

  <%= tab_panel "watch", first: true do %>
    <p>
      The app watches the companies you track at their own careers pages, and records whether each role you track is
      still listed, through one audited write path.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Add by link:</strong> paste a role's link on the Roles page. Its company is added when new; a link on an
        ATS board (Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby, Workday, Oracle) has the board watched at once, read free. The role is checked
        right after when that costs nothing; otherwise "Check now" on its page says what it could cost first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Checks:</strong> "Check now" on a role or a company, or "Check all tracked companies" on the Roles page,
        which confirms the roles you track and finds new ones that fit. A check that could spend says what it could cost
        and asks first: "at most" what its pages cost when last read in full, "about" for a page never read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Descriptions:</strong> read from a role's own page, through its board's job API or the page rendered,
        never by the AI; every version kept, and read again when a check finds the role's own posting elsewhere.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your watch list:</strong> track or archive roles and companies, many at once; archiving a company archives
        its roles in play. A job board's link (LinkedIn, Indeed) is never read: it is kept as where you found a role.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="pages"><%= link_to "Roles", roles_path %> · <%= link_to "Companies", companies_path %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%= tab_panel "find" do %>
    <p>What may fit is proposed, never tracked: you decide what to watch.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Suggestions:</strong> the suggester weighs every watched page's latest roles against your search profile,
        at no cost, and proposes those that fit, each with why; never one you track or archived.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fit:</strong> a role is weighed against your resume when you ask, from its own description, two ways: the
        ATS fit, your resume alone as a recruiter's screening system would read it, and the Background fit beside it. Its
        must-haves are read one by one: one your resume doesn't show caps the ATS fit at 69, and the role's page names what
        you'd likely be screened out on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your background:</strong> on the Profile page, in three parts (what you're looking for, your role
        preferences, background not on your resume), each read only by what may use it, with up to
        <%= ProfileLink::MAX %> of your own links read free. It never raises the ATS fit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Companies:</strong> your AI assistant finds companies that may fit what you want of one, reviews each
        against it with its sources, and proposes them on the Companies page's Suggested tab. Tracking one reads its board
        at once, free; archiving one declines it for good. A company's fit is never weighed into a role's.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="pages"><%= link_to "Profile", profile_path %> · <%= link_to "Roles by fit", roles_path(sort: "fit") %> · <%= link_to "Proposed companies", companies_path(tab: "suggested") %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%= tab_panel "apply" do %>
    <p>Only you start an application or move it: no agent can, whatever it holds.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The pipeline:</strong> Interested, Preparing, Applied, Interviewing, Offer, then a close (accepted,
        rejected, withdrawn, no response), with what happened along the way and with whom.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Writing:</strong> a cover letter or an outreach note, a review of your resume against the role, or a
        resume tailored to it. Only what your resume shows, the lines behind each point, and what the role asks that your
        resume doesn't. Yours to edit and approve; nothing is ever sent from here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Contacts:</strong> the people you deal with at a company, linked to its applications. Their details stay
        in this database; the record names them by a hash alone.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="pages"><%= link_to "Pipeline", applications_path %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%= tab_panel "govern" do %>
    <p>
      Each agent run holds a credential with only the scopes it needs; every write is checked against it, down to what it
      changes, and lands in an append-only audit log. A write no scope allows is refused, on the record.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The review queue:</strong> what an agent can't settle waits for you, with what was seen and an action
        proposed. Your AI assistant can recommend a decision on each; you accept it in one click or decide otherwise, and
        how often you agree is measured per kind.
      </li>
      <li><strong>The Agents page:</strong> who may write what, the credentials their runs held, and every write refused.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>The audit log:</strong> every write on record, yours and every agent's, refused ones included; nothing in
        it can be changed or deleted. Its check results list what a check of all tracked companies found, what needs
        you marked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The dashboard:</strong> where things stand now, and how they have gone: how far applications get, how
        long roles stay listed, what the AI cost, and the runs asked for lately, by you or your AI assistant. A rate
        needs at least <%= Analytics::FLOOR %> to go on, and says so when it hasn't.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="pages">
      <%= link_to "Review", review_path %> · <%= link_to "Agents", agents_path %> · <%= link_to "Audit log", audit_path %> ·
      <%= link_to "Check results", audit_path(view: "checks") %> · <%= link_to "Dashboard", root_path %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%= tab_panel "assistant" do %>
    <p>
      Your own AI assistant (Claude or another MCP client; only Claude Desktop has been tested) works through the app's
      MCP server, on your subscription, and changes the record only on your word.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It writes</strong> letters, notes, resume reviews and tailored resumes from an application's materials and
        the writing rules, and saves them when you say so, each save under a credential for that one call.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It finds and weighs</strong> roles on your watched pages by meaning, and finds and reviews companies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It keeps your watch list</strong> on your word, with your reason on the record; logs what happened and adds
        a dated note to an application's notes, but never moves one; and recommends decisions on the review queue, never
        making one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It answers</strong> from the audit log and the analytics. A role's, an application's or a company's page
        says when it changes what you're looking at, with Refresh.
      </li>
    </ul>
  <% end %>

  </div>

  <p class="muted small">Local and single-user: there is no login, so it is never deployed as it is.</p>
</div>
